  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 505 PM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nSurface high pressure over the western Atlantic will continue to \nbuild through the middle and end of the week. This will result in a \ntighter pressure gradient across Florida and a return of some more \nbrisk east to southeasterly winds. Deeper moisture will also start \nto advect northward into the peninsula, with precipitable water \nvalues looking to climb back up into the 1.8 to 2 inch range. Aloft, \nas the decaying nor'easter over the mid-Atlantic opens up and swings \neast, mid-level ridging will begin to build across the eastern \nseaboard. However, some remnant vorticity from the trough to the \nnorth may make its way down into the Gulf. As such, some guidance \nis hinting at a shortwave disturbance lingering in the northern \nGulf for the Wednesday through Friday period. This feature looks \nto break down as we go into the weekend once ridging continues to \nstrengthen and stretch southward. Overall, chances of \nprecipitation will trend up, about 60 to 70% each afternoon. Storm\nactivity will likely favor interior and southwestern Florida due \nto the stronger easterly flow during the latter half of the week. \nThere is increasing confidence that temperatures will increase \nback into the upper 80s and low 90s areawide over the weekend.",
